<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Allia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 390 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the Allia was fought around 390 BCE, or 387 BCE by Greek reckoning, near the River Allia north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where a Gallic army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brennus-4th-century-bce">Brennus</a> routed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> then sacked Rome, and the day of the defeat, 18 July, was long regarded as unlucky. The disaster prompted the building of stronger defences, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/servian-wall">Servian Wall</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Senones were one of the Gallic tribes that had invaded northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, settling on the Adriatic coast around what is now Rimini. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, they were called to the Etruscan town of Clusium by Aruns, a young man seeking revenge on Lucumo. The Clusians asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> for help, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> sent the three sons of Marcus Fabius Ambustus as ambassadors. A quarrel broke out; the ambassadors joined the fighting, and one killed a Senone chieftain, breaking the rule that ambassadors stay neutral.</p><p>Ancient sources give forces of 24,000 to 40,000 for each side, none of them reliable. Jeremy Armstrong suggests about 5,000 men on each side, while Max Cary and H. H. Scullard placed some 15,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> against a Gallic army of thirty to seventy thousand.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> describe the battle in detail. The armies met where the Allia brook joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a>, 11 Roman miles north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. According to Livy, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> did not set up camp, build a rampart or take the auspices. They stretched their wings to avoid being outflanked, which thinned the line, and placed reserves on a hill on the right. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brennus-4th-century-bce">Brennus</a>, the Senone leader, suspected a ruse and attacked the hill.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> panicked. The left wing threw down its arms and fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a>, where many drowned in their armour, though most reached Veii. The right wing fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>, surprised by the ease of the victory, reached Rome before sunset to find the gates open and the walls unmanned. The Romans withdrew to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitoline Hill</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> says the siege lasted seven months.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The accounts were written centuries after the events. Many modern historians see the rescue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by Camillus as an addition, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> do not mention him. Peter Cornell notes that no archaeological trace of the sack has been found, and suggests the Senones ransacked the city for booty and left after being bought off. A few years later Rome began building new walls 11 km long in yellow Grotta Oscura tuff from a quarry in the territory of Veii.</p><p>The Day of the Allia, 18 July according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>, was regarded as ill-omened and treated as a day on which public and ritual activity was avoided. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> presents this as a lasting civic taboo, but the tradition is attested only from the 1st century BCE and may be a later reconstruction. Modern dates for the battle vary, with c. 387 BCE considered most probable by modern historians.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Allia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 390 BCE, or 387 BCE in Greek reckoning, a Gallic war band defeated the Romans at the <b>Battle of the Allia</b> and sacked the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Livy and Polybius record that part of the people crossed the Alps around 400 BCE; led by Brennus, they defeated the Romans at the <b>Allia</b> and sacked Rome, then settled on the Adriatic coast around Sena Gallica (Senigallia).</blockquote><a href="/state/senones">Senones</a></li><li><blockquote>Roman tradition said Senones led by Brennus defeated Rome at the <b>Allia</b> and sacked the city in 390 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/senones-400">Senones</a></li></ul></section>
